  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Stropharia hornemannii, commonly known as Conifer roundhead, is a small to medium-sized mushroom characterized by its rounded, bell-shaped cap and partial veil. This species belongs to the Strophariaceae family and is notable for its blue-green coloration when young, which fades with age and moisture.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap shape and color",
      "description": "Young fruiting bodies display a distinctive blue-green or blue-gray cap that is rounded to bell-shaped, fading to yellowish or tan with age",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills and spore print",
      "description": "Gills are attached and initially white to pale, becoming dark purple-brown with maturity; spore print is dark purple-brown",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Veil and stem ring",
      "description": "Features a thin partial veil that leaves a delicate, often evanescent ring on the stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Stropharia hornemannii is commonly found in coniferous forests and mixed woodlands throughout New England, fruiting on wood chips, mulch, and disturbed soil near evergreen trees.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The blue-green pigmentation in young specimens is a distinctive feature that makes this species relatively easy to identify in the field, though the color rapidly fades with age and exposure to sunlight.",
